  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": null,
    "grammar": "O'clock follows the number and is used only for exact hours from one to twelve, never with minutes or with the 24-hour clock. Many languages need a word for hour in every time expression; English uses o'clock only at the full hour and often drops it: at six.",
    "culture": "In speech, times are usually given on the 12-hour clock, with in the morning, in the afternoon, or at night added when it matters. The 24-hour clock appears mainly in timetables and never with o'clock.",
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "Two syllables, ə-KLOCK, with the stress on the second; the o is the weak sound ə. The apostrophe is silent but must be written: o'clock, not oclock."
  },
